{"id":"c873733e-63ef-491b-80bf-3dee15a97231","object":"chat.completion","created":1784882713,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # extract fields\\n    items = case.get('items')\\n    required_failure = case.get('required_failure_items')\\n    unresolved_without = case.get('unresolved_without')\\n    if not isinstance(items, list) or not isinstance(required_failure, list) or not isinstance(unresolved_without, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid payload structure')\\n    # convert to sets for fast membership\\n    required_set = set(required_failure)\\n    unresolved_set = set(unresolved_without)\\n    # oracle function\\n    def oracle(subset):\\n        # subset is a list of items (ordered)\\n        # outcome: UNRESOLVED if any unresolved_without item missing from subset\\n        # else FAIL if all required_failure_items present, else PASS\\n        subset_set = set(subset)\\n        for item in unresolved_set:\\n            if item not in subset_set:\\n                return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        if required_set.issubset(subset_set):\\n            return 'FAIL'\\n        return 'PASS'\\n\\n    test_count = 0\\n    def test(subset):\\n        nonlocal test_count\\n        test_count += 1\\n        return oracle(subset)\\n\\n    # start with full set\\n    current = list(items)\\n    outcome = test(current)\\n    if outcome != 'FAIL':\\n        raise ValueError('Initial configuration must produce FAIL')\\n\\n    n = 2\\n    while n <= len(current):\\n        # partition current into n contiguous subsets\\n        base_len = len(current) // n\\n        subsets = []\\n        start = 0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if i < n - 1:\\n                end = start + base_len\\n            else:\\n                end = len(current)\\n            subsets.append(current[start:end])\\n            start = end\\n\\n        reduced = False\\n        # test subsets first\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            if test(subset) == 'FAIL':\\n                current = subset\\n                n = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n        if reduced:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # test complements\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            complement = [item for item in current if item not in subset]\\n            if not complement:\\n                continue\\n            if test(complement) == 'FAIL':\\n                current = complement\\n                n = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n        if reduced:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # no reduction: increase granularity\\n        n = min(n * 2, len(current))\\n        if n == len(current):\\n            # next iteration will try n == len (singletons) then double beyond\\n            pass\\n\\n    # final minimal set is guaranteed FAIL\\n    # test again to respect counting? Already counted during loop, but we can record outcome\\n    return {\\n        'minimal_failure': current,\\n        'outcome': 'FAIL',\\n        'tests': test_count\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-DD-01 based on the description. The task is to implement a ddmin (delta debugging) variant with \"unresolved\" semantics. The case payload has: items (list of strings), required_failure_items (list of strings), unresolved_without (list of strings). The procedure: We have a set of items; we want to find a minimal subset (ordered configuration?) that still produces FAIL. The ddmin algorithm typically splits into subsets, tests each subset and its complement. Here we have three outcomes: PASS, FAIL, UNRESOLVED. The description says: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" Also \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" And \"Return the 1-minimal failure, test count, and terminal outcome.\"\n\nWe need to interpret the semantics. The payload includes required_failure_items: items that must be present for a FAIL? Possibly the oracle function: given a subset of items, it returns FAIL if all required_failure_items are in the subset? Or maybe it's more nuanced because of unresolved_without. The description says: \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL.\" So we can assume that the full set of items (the whole list) will produce FAIL. Also we have unresolved_without: items that cause UNRESOLVED if absent? Possibly the oracle is defined as: Given a set S of items (subset of items), the outcome is:\n- FAIL if all required_failure_items are present in S (maybe also none of the unresolved_without items? but unresolved_without says \"unresolved_without\" so if those items are missing, outcome is UNRESOLVED? Actually \"unresolved_without: list of items that cause UNRESOLVED when absent\"? That is a guess. Let's look at the fixture cases:\n\nCase 0: required_failure_items: [\"d2\",\"d3\"], unresolved_without: []. So full set should be FAIL. Subsets that include both d2 and d3 are FAIL; those missing at least one are PASS? But unresolved_without empty so UNRESOLVED never occurs.\n\nCase 1: required_failure_items: [\"d3\",\"d4\"], unresolved_without: [\"d0\"]. So if subset does not include d0, outcome UNRESOLVED? Or if subset includes d0 then normal? The description \"unresolved_without\" suggests that when those items are absent, the test outcome is UNRESOLVED (neither PASS nor FAIL). So oracle: if any item from unresolved_without is not in S, then UNRESOLVED. Else if all required_failure_items are in S, then FAIL, else PASS.\n\nCase 2: required_failure_items: [\"d4\",\"d7\"], unresolved_without: [\"d0\"]. Similar.\n\nSo we can define an oracle function that takes a set of items (subset) and returns one of \"PASS\", \"FAIL\", \"UNRESOLVED\". \n\nNow implement ddmin with these outcomes. The algorithm:\n\nWe start with the full set (all items). It must produce FAIL. Then we try to reduce. The typical ddmin algorithm (Zeller) works by splitting the current set into subsets and testing each. But here we have UNRESOLVED, and the rule: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" Also \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements.\" And \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\"\n\nInterpretation: We maintain a current set of items that causes FAIL. We try to find a smaller subset that still fails. We use a granularity parameter n (initially 2? Or maybe the size of the current set?). Typically ddmin starts with n=2 and splits into n subsets. But the description says \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That might mean: after we successfully reduce the set (i.e., find a smaller subset that fails), we reduce the granularity (maybe divide by something? Actually \"decrease granularity\" means make it finer, i.e., smaller subsets? Usually in ddmin, after a reduction we reset the granularity to 2. But here they say \"double it up to current size.\" Possibly: we start with granularity = 2. For each iteration, we partition the current set into n subsets (n = granularity). We test each subset. If a subset fails, we reduce current set to that subset and reset granularity to 2 (or decrease). If none fails, we test complements? \"test subsets before complements\" suggests we first test each subset, and if none fails, then we test complements? Or maybe we test subsets first, and if any subset fails we reduce; otherwise we test the complement of each subset? Actually typical ddmin tests subsets, and if any fails, reduce; else test complements. But here we have UNRESOLVED outcomes: if a subset is UNRESOLVED, we cannot reduce on it (only FAIL). So if a subset is UNRESOLVED, we might skip? Or we might still test its complement? The description: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So if a subset is PASS or UNRESOLVED, we don't reduce. But we still might test complements? The typical algorithm tests subsets first; if no subset fails, then test complements (i.e., the set minus each subset). If any complement fails, reduce to that complement. If no complement fails, increase granularity (double it) and continue.\n\nBut \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" So after a reduction, we decrease granularity (maybe set to 2). If no reduction in a round (i.e., no subset nor complement fails), then we double granularity, but not exceed the current set size. Then repeat until granularity > len(current set) (or maybe until no more reductions possible, and granularity > size?).\n\nWe need to implement \"ddmin_with_unresolved\". The algorithm steps:\n\n1. Start with the full list of items as an ordered list? The items are ordered (index). We need to preserve ordering? ddmin on ordered configurations: we treat the configuration as a sequence and we minimize the set by removing contiguous chunks? Actually ddmin on sets (unordered) uses subsets; but here items are given as a list, maybe order matters for the \"subset/complement schedule\"? The problem says \"Minimize a failure-inducing ordered configuration\". So we treat the items as an ordered sequence. The ddmin algorithm for ordered configurations splits the sequence into contiguous intervals. That is the classic ddmin algorithm for minimizing test cases (like a sequence of input characters). So we need to treat the items as an ordered list. The subsets and complements are contiguous intervals? Actually the typical ddmin for ordered input: you split the input into n intervals (first n-1 of equal size, last may be smaller). Then you test each interval (subset), and also the complement (the rest). The minimization is over contiguous deletions.\n\nGiven the description: \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements.\" And \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" And \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" The payload has \"items\" (ordered list). So we treat items as an ordered sequence; we want to find a minimal contiguous subsequence that still fails? Or minimal set of items not necessarily contiguous? The term \"ordered configuration\" might mean the items are ordered, but the subsets can be any subset (order doesn't matter for test outcome) but we use the ordering for partitioning. In many ddmin contexts, the input is a sequence of changes (like deltas) and we try to find a minimal set of changes that causes failure. The ordering is used to divide into intervals. The algorithm does not require that the minimal set be contiguous; it can find non-contiguous items through the process of testing subsets and complements.\n\nLet's review: The classic ddmin algorithm (Zeller & Hildebrandt) for minimizing failing test cases works on a set of changes (like a list of changes). It partitions the set into n subsets, and tests each subset. If any subset fails, that subset becomes the new set and n is reset to 2. If no subset fails, then test the complements. If any complement fails, that complement becomes the new set and n is reset to 2. If none fails, increase n (double, up to |S|). Process continues until granularity exceeds size. The result is a 1-minimal set (no single element can be removed without causing PASS). The outcome semantics: only FAIL (i.e., the test fails) allows reduction. In our case, we have UNRESOLVED outcomes as well. The description says \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So when testing a subset or complement, if outcome is UNRESOLVED, we treat it like PASS (no reduction). But we still need to handle the algorithm: if a subset returns UNRESOLVED, we cannot reduce to it. But we might still test complements; if a complement returns FAIL, we can reduce. However, if a subset is UNRESOLVED, does that affect behavior? Possibly we still need to test complements? Standard ddmin tests all subsets first; if any subset fails, we reduce and restart with n=2. If no subset fails, we then test complements. But here, if a subset is UNRESOLVED, we don't reduce, but we still need to consider complements. The complement might be FAIL. So the algorithm should not skip testing complements just because a subset was UNRESOLVED.\n\nHowever, there is nuance: In classic ddmin, when you test a subset and it is PASS, you still test its complement because the failure might be in the complement. But if a subset is UNRESOLVED, does that affect the complement? The complement might be FAIL, PASS, or UNRESOLVED. So we should test complements after all subsets regardless of subset outcomes, but only if no subset was FAIL. Because if a subset was FAIL, we reduce and restart, so we never test complements in that round. So the algorithm is: For each round with granularity n:\n   Partition current set S into n subsets (contiguous intervals: first n-1 of size floor(len(S)/n), last one size = len(S) - (n-1)*base_size).\n   For each subset in order:\n        Test subset. If outcome == FAIL:\n            set S = subset, n = 2 (or decrease granularity), and start new round (break out of subset loop).\n   If no subset was FAIL:\n        For each subset (in order):\n            Test complement (i.e., S minus that subset). If outcome == FAIL:\n                set S = complement, n = 2, start new round.\n   If no complement was FAIL either:\n        increase n: n = min(n*2, len(S))? Actually \"double it up to current size\" means set n = n*2, but if n*2 > len(S), set n = len(S). Then continue with same S. If n > len(S) (or maybe if n == len(S) and still no reduction), then stop. But typical ddmin stops when n > len(S). So we stop when n > len(S).\nThe result is the minimal S (list of items) that causes FAIL. We then need to return the \"minimal_failure\" as a list of strings (the items in the minimal set, presumably in the order they appear in original items? The output schema says \"minimal_failure\" is array of strings. So we return the ordered list of items in the minimal set (preserving original order? Probably yes, as they are in the minimal configuration). Also return \"outcome\" string, which is the outcome of the minimal set (should be \"FAIL\" because we only proceed if initial FAIL and we only reduce on FAIL, so final set must be FAIL). And \"tests\" integer: the number of oracle calls made during the algorithm (the number of times we invoked the oracle function). We must count all tests performed (including initial test? The initial configuration must produce FAIL; we should test it to verify? The description: \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL.\" So we can assume it does, but we might still test it and count it. But typically we start with the full set and test it to confirm. We'll include that as test 1. Then each subset and complement test is counted.\n\nNow implement the oracle function given the case payload. We need to determine the outcome for a given subset (list of items) based on required_failure_items and unresolved_without. The rule: If any item from unresolved_without is missing from the subset, outcome = \"UNRESOLVED\". Else if all required_failure_items are present, outcome = \"FAIL\". Else outcome = \"PASS\". This matches the examples: For case 0, unresolved_without empty, so no UNRESOLVED. For case1: if subset does not include d0, it's UNRESOLVED. If includes d0 and includes both d3,d4 -> FAIL, else PASS. That seems plausible.\n\nNow implement solve(case) that takes the payload dict and returns dict with keys: minimal_failure, outcome, tests. Handle edge cases: If the initial full set is not FAIL, raise ValueError. Also if required_failure_items are not subset of items? Possibly not.\n\nWe need to output Python source code as a JSON string. The code should define solve(case) using only standard library.\n\nKey steps in code:\n- Extract items list, required_failure set, unresolved_without set.\n- Define oracle(subset): given list of items (subset in original order or set? For outcome we only need membership, but we need to preserve order for output. We'll keep subset as list (subset of original items, maintaining original order). For oracle, convert to set for quick membership.\n- Initialize current = items (full list).\n- test_count = 0\n- function test(subset): returns outcome and increments test_count.\n   outcome = oracle(subset)\n   test_count += 1\n   return outcome\n- First test full set; if not FAIL: raise ValueError.\n- Then run ddmin loop:\n   n = 2\n   while n <= len(current):\n        base_len = len(current) // n\n        subsets = []\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            if i < n-1:\n                end = start + base_len\n            else:\n                end = len(current)\n            subset = current[start:end]\n            subsets.append(subset)\n            start = end\n        # Test subsets\n        reduced = False\n        for subset in subsets:\n            outcome = test(subset)\n            if outcome == \"FAIL\":\n                current = subset\n                n = 2  # decrease granularity (reset to 2)\n                reduced = True\n                break\n        if reduced:\n            continue\n        # Test complements\n        for subset in subsets:\n            complement = [item for item in current if item not in subset]  # but note: subset is contiguous, complement is the rest (not contiguous if we just remove that interval). However classic ddmin uses complement as the current set minus the subset (the rest). That is correct. But we need to preserve order? Complement should be the items of current not in subset, preserving original order. Since current is a list, we can filter.\n            if not complement:  # skip empty complement? But subset could be whole current if n=1? But n>=2 so subsets are non-empty, complement may be empty if subset is whole? Actually if n=2 and current size even, each subset half; complement of first half is second half, non-empty. But if n=1 not used. So complement non-empty.\n                continue\n            outcome = test(complement)\n            if outcome == \"FAIL\":\n                current = complement\n                n = 2\n                reduced = True\n                break\n        if not reduced:\n            n = min(n*2, len(current))\n            if n == len(current):  # if doubling does nothing, maybe break to avoid infinite loop? But if n == len(current) and no reduction, then next iteration n will be > len(current) because n*2 > len? Actually after n = len(current), the while condition is n <= len(current) -> true, so we'll do another round with n=len(current). That means we partition into len(current) subsets of size 1 each. Test each singleton; if none fails (they are PASS or UNRESOLVED), then test complements (each complement is all but one item). That's the final check for 1-minimal. If still no reduction, then n becomes 2*len(current) which is > len(current), so loop ends. So we don't need special break.\n\n   After loop, current is minimal failing set.\n   outcome_final = test(current)  # should be FAIL, but we can test again? Might be redundant but okay. But we already know it's FAIL because we only ever set current to something that was FAIL. But we can record outcome_final as \"FAIL\".\n   Return {\"minimal_failure\": current, \"outcome\": \"FAIL\", \"tests\": test_count}.\n\nOne nuance: In testing complements, if a subset is empty? Not possible. But if the complement is empty (when subset is the whole current set), then we skip because empty set cannot be tested? Possibly we could test but it's not a meaningful subset. The algorithm typically doesn't test empty set. So skip.\n\nAlso note: The items are strings, and the minimal_failure should be the list of strings in order as they appear in current (which preserves original order since we never reorder). That is fine.\n\nNow we need to write the Python code as a string, escaping for JSON. We'll produce the JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value that string.\n\nWe must ensure no external imports beyond standard library.\n\nLet's write the code in a clear way. We'll define solve(case) that extracts payload, calls a helper function _ddmin. Return dict.\n\nPotential edge: case may have additional keys beyond items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. We should ignore others.\n\nWe'll validate: items must be list, required_failure_items list, unresolved_without list. If missing or invalid types, raise ValueError.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1259,"completion_tokens":4585,"total_tokens":5844,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3903},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":0,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":1259},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}